	shorttitle = {Nebula},
	abstract = {3D Gaussian splatting (3DGS) has drawn significant attention in the architectural community recently. However, current architectural designs often overlook the 3DGS scalability, making them fragile for extremely large-scale 3DGS. Meanwhile, the {VR} bandwidth requirement makes it impossible to deliver high-fidelity and smooth {VR} content from the cloud.
	We present Nebula, a coherent acceleration framework for large-scale 3DGS collaborative rendering. Instead of streaming videos, Nebula streams intermediate results after the {LoD} search, reducing 1925\% data communication between the cloud and the client. To further enhance the motion-to-photon experience, we introduce a temporal-aware {LoD} search in the cloud that tames the irregular memory access and reduces redundant data access by exploiting temporal coherence across frames. On the client side, we propose a novel stereo rasterization that enables two eyes to share most computations during the stereo rendering with bit-accurate quality. With minimal hardware augmentations, Nebula achieves 2.7\${\textbackslash}times\$ motion-to-photon speedup and reduces 1925\% bandwidth over lossy video streaming.},
